description:
persona-attestors/src/ssh.rs:289-294:

    let mut len_buf = [0u8; 4];
    stream.read_exact(&mut len_buf).await?;
    let resp_len = u32::from_be_bytes(len_buf) as usize;
    let mut resp = vec![0u8; resp_len];
    stream.read_exact(&mut resp).await?;

resp_len is taken straight from the wire with no upper bound and the buffer is allocated and zeroed before any of the body has arrived. A peer that writes the four bytes FF FF FF FF causes an immediate 4 GiB zeroed allocation.

Trigger: SSH_AUTH_SOCK points at something that is not a well-behaved OpenSSH agent. This is an inherited environment variable that personad reads at enumerate() time (ssh.rs:317-318), and the socket it names is chosen by whatever launched the daemon -- an agent-forwarded session, a third-party agent implementation, or any same-user process that binds a socket at that path. is_available() (ssh.rs:262-267) only checks that the path exists; nothing verifies what is behind it.

What goes wrong: personad aborts on allocation failure or is OOM-killed, taking down identity issuance for every consumer on the machine. Because enumerate() runs on every FetchJWTSVID (service.rs:121-122), the trigger is reachable on any request, not just at startup. A truncated or non-agent response also leaves read_exact blocking with no timeout.

For reference, the SSH agent protocol caps messages at 256 KiB and OpenSSH own client rejects anything larger, so a bound here costs nothing in compatibility.

Fix direction: reject resp_len above a fixed maximum (256 KiB) before allocating, and consider read-into-a-growing-buffer plus an overall timeout on agent_roundtrip.
